# Natural Hazards and Disaster Management

> Earthquakes strike without warning, cyclones announce themselves for
> days, and floods can rise in hours. This lesson sorts the hazards,
> maps India's vulnerable zones, and walks through the disaster
> management cycle that turns vulnerability into resilience.

## 1. Hazard and Disaster: A Critical Difference

[definition]
A **hazard** is a potentially damaging natural event or process; a
**disaster** is what happens when such an event overwhelms a
community's capacity to cope, causing widespread loss of life,
property, and livelihood.
[/definition]

- An earthquake in an empty desert is a hazard; the same quake under
  a crowded city is a disaster.
- **Vulnerability** — the exposure of people and buildings — decides
  the scale of loss as much as the hazard itself, so risk grows where
  unsafe construction meets a dangerous coast or fault line.

## 2. Families of Natural Hazards

@diagram tree id="hazard-families" layout="tree" aria="Families of natural hazards with examples"
  "Natural hazards" -> Geophysical
