  "Natural hazards" -> Climatological
  "Natural hazards" -> Biological
  Geophysical -> "Earthquakes, tsunamis, landslides, volcanoes"
  Hydrological -> "Floods, flash floods, avalanches"
  Meteorological -> "Cyclones, thunderstorms, heatwaves"
  Climatological -> "Droughts, wildfires"
  Biological -> "Epidemics, pest attacks"
@/diagram

## 3. Earthquakes and India's Seismic Map

[important]
The Bureau of Indian Standards divides the country into four seismic
zones — II, III, IV, and V — with Zone V the most severe; roughly
three-fifths of the landmass lies in moderate to high risk zones.
[/important]

| Zone | Risk | Typical areas |
| --- | --- | --- |
| V | Highest | Entire north-east, Kutch, Himachal, Uttarakhand, north Bihar, Andaman and Nicobar |
| IV | High | Delhi and remaining Himalayan tracts |
| III | Moderate | Kathiawar peninsula and much of the Deccan rim |
| II | Low | Stable peninsular interior |

Historic shocks: the Latur-Killari quake of 1993 took about 9,000
lives in the stable Deccan, far from any plate boundary, and the
**Bhuj earthquake of 26 January 2001** (magnitude about 7.7, some
20,000 dead) forced a nationwide rewrite of building codes.

## 4. Cyclones, Floods, and Droughts

- Tropical cyclones form over warm seas; the Bay of Bengal breeds
  far more storms than the Arabian Sea, so the east coast — Odisha,
  Andhra Pradesh, Tamil Nadu, West Bengal — is hit hardest.
- Cyclonic storms begin near 62 km/h winds, and **super cyclones**
  exceed about 220 km/h; the storm surge, not wind alone, is the
  great killer of coastal populations.
- The India Meteorological Department and a WMO/ESCAP panel of
  countries name the storms, and warnings use green, yellow, orange,
  and red colour codes.
- Floods: about 40 million hectares are flood-prone; the Brahmaputra
  and the shifting Kosi, the "sorrow of Bihar", flood every year,
  while cities such as Mumbai (2005) and Chennai (2015) showed how
  clogged drains turn heavy rain into urban disaster.
- Drought: roughly one-third of India is drought-prone; a shortfall
  is meteorological at first and becomes agricultural when soils and
  crops dry out.

@chart bar title="Approximate lives lost in major Indian disasters" labels:
Latur quake 1993
