@/chart

[example]
Compare Odisha's super cyclone of 1999 with Phailin in 2013: storms
of comparable power, yet evacuating about a million people cut
Phailin's toll to a few dozen — proof that preparedness saves more
lives than any dam or embankment.
[/example]

## 5. Landslides, Tsunamis, and Other Quiet Killers

- Landslides concentrate in the Himalaya and the Western Ghats, where
  steep slopes, tremors, and road-cutting meet heavy rain (Malin,
  2014; Wayanad, 2024).
- The Indian Ocean tsunami of 26 December 2004, born of a magnitude
  about 9.1 quake off Sumatra, killed some 10,700 people in India —
  mostly in Tamil Nadu, Kerala, Andhra Pradesh, and the Andamans —
  and led to the Indian Tsunami Early Warning Centre of 2007 at
  Hyderabad.
- Lightning and heatwaves now rank among the deadliest everyday
  hazards; simple rules — shelter indoors, keep hydrated, avoid open
  fields during storms — save thousands of lives each year.

## 6. The Disaster Management Cycle

@diagram tree id="dm-cycle" layout="tree" aria="The four phases of the disaster management cycle"
  "Disaster management cycle" -> Mitigation
  "Disaster management cycle" -> Preparedness
  "Disaster management cycle" -> Response
  "Disaster management cycle" -> Recovery
  Mitigation -> "Land-use zoning, building codes, embankments"
  Preparedness -> "Early warning, mock drills, relief stockpiles"
  Response -> "Search and rescue, relief camps, medical aid"
  Recovery -> "Rehabilitation and rebuilding back better"
@/diagram

- The **Disaster Management Act, 2005** created the NDMA, chaired by
  the Prime Minister, above State (SDMA) and District (DDMA)
  authorities, supported by specialised NDRF battalions trained for
  search and rescue.
- India follows the **Sendai Framework (2015-2030)** for reducing
  disaster risk and marks the International Day for Disaster Risk
  Reduction every 13 October.

[tip]
Remember the cycle order — mitigation and preparedness come *before*
the event, response and recovery *after* it; examiners love asking
which phase a given action belongs to.
[/tip]

## 7. Community Resilience: Case Studies

- Odisha built hundreds of cyclone shelters and one of the first
  state disaster authorities after 1999; its evacuation model is now
  cited across the world.
- Kerala's floods of 2018 showed the power of community rescue: the
  fishing fleet became the state's "own army" and coordination ran
  through strong local networks.
- Schemes such as Aapda Mitra train community volunteers, and school
  safety mock drills recognise that the first responder is usually
  the person standing beside you.

[warning]
During an earthquake, doorways and rushing downstairs are myths of
safety; Drop-Cover-Hold on under a sturdy table indoors, and move to
open ground only after the shaking stops, watching for aftershocks.
[/warning]

## 8. Quick Revision

| Hazard | India's hotspots | Key safety or policy tool |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Earthquake | Zone V: north-east, Kutch, Himalaya | BIS building codes, Drop-Cover-Hold |
| Cyclone | East coast facing the Bay of Bengal | IMD colour warnings, cyclone shelters |
| Flood | Brahmaputra-Kosi plains and cities | Embankments, drainage, flood-plain zoning |
| Drought | About one-third of the country | Watershed works, drought-resistant crops |
| Landslide | Himalaya and Western Ghats | Slope treatment and hazard zonation |
| Tsunami | East coast and Andaman and Nicobar | Indian warning centre at Hyderabad, 2007 |
